+## Pitfalls
+
+- **Gateway not running** — webhooks require the Hermes gateway to be active. If `curl localhost:8644/health` fails, start the gateway first.
+- **Webhook platform not enabled** — running `hermes webhook list` will tell you. Enable via `hermes gateway setup` or manual config.
+- **Wrong HMAC header name** — GitHub sends `X-Hub-Signature-256`, GitLab sends `X-Gitlab-Token`. The adapter handles both, but verify your service's header format matches.
+- **Secret mismatch** — if the secret in your external service doesn't match the one from `hermes webhook list`, all POSTs will be rejected with 401.
+- **Firewall/NAT blocking** — the webhook URL must be reachable from the external service. For local development, use a tunnel (ngrok, cloudflared).
+- **Event type mismatch** — if `--events` filter doesn't match what the service sends, the webhook will be silently ignored. Use `hermes webhook test <name>` to verify.
+- **Prompt template field errors** — if `{field.path}` doesn't exist in the payload, it renders as empty string. Test with real payloads first.
+- **deliver-only without --deliver** — `--deliver-only` requires a real delivery target. `--deliver log` is rejected.
+- **Subscription not persisting** — subscriptions are saved to `~/.hermes/webhook_subscriptions.json`. If this file is deleted or permissions are wrong, subscriptions are lost.
+- **Rate limiting** — high-volume webhooks may hit rate limits. Consider batching or using `--deliver-only` for high-frequency events.
+
